<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1870</h1><p class="ref-lede">1870 in world history: 41 events, 18 births, 15 deaths, 6 works, 2 states founded and 5 that came to an end. 1,013 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a><a href="/years/1867">1867</a><a href="/years/1868">1868</a><a href="/years/1869">1869</a><a href="/years/1870" aria-current="page">1870</a><a href="/years/1871">1871</a><a href="/years/1872">1872</a><a href="/years/1873">1873</a><a href="/years/1874">1874</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-3">3 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Construction of the Brooklyn Bridge begins</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Work started on John Roebling's suspension bridge across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>'s East River. Completed in 1883 under his son Washington and daughter-in-law Emily, it was then the longest suspension bridge in the world.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Thomas Nast's cartoon links the Democrats with a donkey</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Nast's cartoon 'A Live Jackass Kicking a Dead Lion' in Harper's Weekly portrayed anti-war Democrats as a donkey. The donkey later became the accepted symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US Army troops commit the Marias Massacre in Montana</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army forces under Major Eugene Mortimer Baker attacked Piegan Blackfeet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> Territory, killing approximately 200 people, most of them women, children and older men. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marias-massacre">Marias Massacre</a> was part of the Indian Wars.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marias-massacre">Marias Massacre</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-3">3 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Fifteenth Amendment ratified, barring racial bars to voting</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The amendment forbade denying citizens the vote on grounds of race, colour or previous servitude. Southern states later evaded it with poll taxes, literacy tests and intimidation for almost a century.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Hiram Revels becomes the first African American US Senator</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> minister was sworn in after a fierce debate over his eligibility. He filled the seat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a> had left when the South seceded.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-9">9 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Charles Dickens dies at Gad's Hill</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Dickens died at his Kent home aged 58, leaving The Mystery of Edwin Drood unfinished. His novels of poverty and injustice had made him the most popular author of his age.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-18">18 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The First Vatican Council defines papal infallibility</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The council adopted Pastor aeternus, declaring the Pope free from error when he defines doctrine on faith or morals ex cathedra. The decree strengthened papal authority and caused a schism with the Old Catholics.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">France declares war on Prussia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Provoked by Bismarck's edited Ems Dispatch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s government declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Within months <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was defeated, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> fell and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was proclaimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Tower Subway opens beneath the Thames</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A cable-hauled railway carriage began carrying passengers through the Tower Subway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Bored with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunnelling-shield">tunnelling shield</a>, it was the world's first tube railway, though it closed within months.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-6">6 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Louisa Ann Swain casts her ballot in Laramie, Wyoming</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Louisa Ann Swain voted in a general election in Laramie, Wyoming. She was the first woman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to do so since the repeal of women's suffrage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> in 1807.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-20">20 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Italian troops breach the walls of Rome at Porta Pia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After French troops left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Italian forces broke through the walls and took the city. The Papal States ended, and Rome became the capital of united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the following year.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sedan-1870" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sedan-1870.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sedan-1870">Battle of Sedan (1870)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Sedan was fought on 1–2 September 1870 in the Franco-Prussian War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Campaign</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870">Red River Expedition (1870)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Red River Expedition of 1870 was a military force of British regulars and Canadian militia led by Colonel Garnet Wolseley to assert Canadian authority at Red River, in present-day Manitoba, after the resistance led by Louis Riel.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelotte" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelotte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelotte">Battle of Gravelotte</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Gravelotte, also called Gravelotte–St. Privat, was fought on 18 August 1870 west of Metz and was the largest battle of the Franco-Prussian War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worth" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-worth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worth">Battle of Wörth</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Wörth, or Frœschwiller-Wœrth, was fought on 6 August 1870 in northern Alsace in the opening weeks of the Franco-Prussian War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba-act" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manitoba-act.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/manitoba-act">Manitoba Act</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Manitoba Act, passed by the Parliament of Canada in May 1870, created the province of Manitoba from part of Rupert's Land after the Red River Resistance.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-okahandja">Peace of Okahandja</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Peace of Okahandja was the settlement of 1870, negotiated with the help of German missionaries, that ended a decade of war between the Herero under Maharero and the Oorlam Afrikaners under Jan Jonker Afrikaner in central Namibia.</p></div></li></ol></section>
